11. Production engineering improvements
Before using this architecture with real enterprise documents, understand these six improvements:
Area	Current learning implementation	Production direction
Retrieval	Top-K vector search	Hybrid search and reranking
Relevance	No score threshold	Evaluate and tune relevance thresholds
Document ingestion	Synchronous upload	Background workers and job queues
Storage	Local ChromaDB	Managed or durable vector storage
Security	User metadata filtering	Tenant-aware authorization and isolation
Observability	Basic error messages	Logs, traces, token and latency metrics
A further important issue is source attribution: the API returns retrieved sources, but it does not yet verify that every citation written by the LLM corresponds to a source that actually supports the claim. That requires citation validation.
Also, your Day 20 RAG requests are not yet being recorded in the PostgreSQL usage-history table. That will be an important platform enhancement.
12. AI Engineer interview preparation
Practice answering these questions aloud.
Q1. What is RAG and why is it useful?
RAG retrieves relevant external information and supplies it to an LLM as context before generation. It helps applications answer questions using private, domain-specific and frequently updated information.
Q2. What is the difference between RAG and fine-tuning?
RAG	Fine-tuning
Retrieves external information	Updates model behaviour through training
Knowledge can be updated by changing documents	Updating knowledge may require further training
Provides source references	Does not inherently provide source references
Useful for document Q&A	Useful for specialized behaviour, style and task adaptation
Q3. What happens during a RAG query?
Question embedding → vector retrieval → context assembly → LLM generation → answer with source references.
Q4. What is top-K retrieval?
It retrieves the K most relevant results from the vector database. For example, top_k=3 retrieves three candidate passages.
Q5. What is the difference between a retriever and a generator?
A retriever finds relevant information. A generator uses that information to produce a natural-language response.
Q6. How do you reduce hallucinations in RAG?
•	Improve document extraction and chunking.
•	Use relevant retrieval and reranking.
•	Provide clear grounding instructions.
•	Return an explicit insufficient-context response.
•	Validate citations.
•	Evaluate answers against known reference questions.
Q7. How would you scale this system to 10,000 users?
I would use:
•	FastAPI services behind a load balancer.
•	Redis or RabbitMQ for asynchronous ingestion.
•	Object storage for original documents.
•	PostgreSQL for users, metadata and request history.
•	A scalable vector database.
•	Batch embedding generation.
•	Authentication, authorization and tenant isolation.
•	Monitoring for latency, token consumption, failures and retrieval quality.
